cisions that can change without damaging it. That distinction helps a production protect its central idea while still adapting to technical problems, schedule pressure, feedback, or distribution needs.\n\n## Collaboration and iteration\n\nCreative work becomes more reliable when feedback is attached to a clear objective. A note such as \"make it better\" is difficult to act on. A note about pacing, clarity, continuity, tone, audience understanding, technical quality, or a specific rights problem can be tested.\n\nIteration also changes with production stage. Early revisions are cheap and broad. Late revisions are usually narrower because more departments and assets depend on earlier choices. This is why experienced productions try to identify structural problems before expensive finishing work begins.\n\n## Evaluating the result\n\nFor radio production, useful evaluation criteria include clarity, timing, pacing, audio quality, audience flow, reliability, and compliance. These criteria do not replace artistic judgement. They make judgement more accountable by asking what the work is trying to achieve and whether the production choices support that aim.\n\nAudience response can add another layer of evidence, but popularity and quality are not identical. A project can be technically accomplished and reach a small audience, or attract a large audience while still containing craft problems. Good evaluation separates commercial outcomes, audience outcomes, and creative outcomes instead of treating them as one score.\n\n## Rights, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include broadcast standards, music licensing, safe levels, contributor consent, and emergency procedures. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nRadio production becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Radio production: Where creative choices change outcomes","word_count":636,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Radio production"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"EBU","title":"EBU","url":"https://www.ebu.ch/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"entertainment-archive-2026-088","published_at":"2026-04-25T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"radio-production-how-quality-is-evaluated-088","title":"Radio production: How quality is evaluated","description":"Radio production explained through how quality is evaluated, with craft, production, evaluation, rights, safety, and audience context.","body_markdown":"# Radio production: How quality is evaluated\n\nRadio production sits inside a larger entertainment system of creative intent, production craft, collaboration, technology, rights, distribution, and audience response. This article focuses on how quality is evaluated while keeping those connections visible.\n\n## The core idea\n\nCreating audio programmes through scripting, presenting, recording, live control, editing, music, and transmission. Professional work rarely comes from one isolated decision. It emerges from a chain of choices that need to remain coherent as the project moves between people, tools, locations, and deadlines.\n\nThe practical vocabulary of radio production includes scripting, microphone technique, cueing, live mixing, editing, timing, music scheduling, and transmission workflows. These elements are useful because they let collaborators discuss the work precisely instead of relying only on taste or intuition.\n\n## How quality is evaluated\n\nQuality is evaluated through a combination of artistic intention, technical execution, consistency, audience comprehension, and production reliability. Useful indicators for radio production include clarity, timing, pacing, audio quality, audience flow, reliability, and compliance. No single metric can capture creative value, but clear criteria make feedback more specific and revisions more productive.\n\n## Creative intention meets production reality\n\nEntertainment production is a negotiation between ambition and constraints. Time, budget, access, performer availability, technology, rights, platform requirements, and audience expectations can all change what is possible. Constraints do not automatically reduce quality. Often they force a project to decide what matters most.\n\nThe strongest teams make those trade-offs explicit. They distinguish decisions that define the identity of the work from decisions that can change without damaging it. That distinction helps a production protect its central idea while still adapting to technical problems, schedule pressure, feedback, or distribution needs.\n\n## Collaboration and iteration\n\nCreative work becomes more reliable when feedback is attached to a clear objective. A note such as \"make it better\" is difficult to act on. A note about pacing, clarity, continuity, tone, audience understanding, technical quality, or a specific rights problem can be tested.\n\nIteration also changes with production stage. Early revisions are cheap and broad. Late revisions are usually narrower because more departments and assets depend on earlier choices. This is why experienced productions try to identify structural problems before expensive finishing work begins.\n\n## Evaluating the result\n\nFor radio production, useful evaluation criteria include clarity, timing, pacing, audio quality, audience flow, reliability, and compliance. These criteria do not replace artistic judgement. They make judgement more accountable by asking what the work is trying to achieve and whether the production choices support that aim.\n\nAudience response can add another layer of evidence, but popularity and quality are not identical. A project can be technically accomplished and reach a small audience, or attract a large audience while still containing craft problems. Good evaluation separates commercial outcomes, audience outcomes, and creative outcomes instead of treating them as one score.\n\n## Rights, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include broadcast standards, music licensing, safe levels, contributor consent, and emergency procedures. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nRadio production becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Radio production: How quality is evaluated","word_count":636,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Radio production"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"EBU","title":"EBU","url":"https://www.ebu.ch/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"entertainment-archive-2026-089","published_at":"2026-04-26T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"radio-production-what-beginners-often-misunderstand-089","title":"Radio production: What beginners often misunderstand","description":"Radio production explained through what beginners often misunderstand, with craft, production, evaluation, rights, safety, and audience context.","body_markdown":"# Radio production: What beginners often misunderstand\n\nRadio production sits inside a larger entertainment system of creative intent, production craft, collaboration, technology, rights, distribution, and audience response. This article focuses on what beginners often misunderstand while keeping those connections visible.\n\n## The core idea\n\nCreating audio programmes through scripting, presenting, recording, live control, editing, music, and transmission. Professional work rarely comes from one isolated decision. It emerges from a chain of choices that need to remain coherent as the project moves between people, tools, locations, and deadlines.\n\nThe practical vocabulary of radio production includes scripting, microphone technique, cueing, live mixing, editing, timing, music scheduling, and transmission workflows. These elements are useful because they let collaborators discuss the work precisely instead of relying only on taste or intuition.\n\n## What beginners often misunderstand\n\nBeginners often assume that professional results come mainly from expensive tools, inspiration, or one signature technique. In practice, radio production depends on preparation, iteration, collaboration, and disciplined control of basics. Scripting, microphone technique, cueing, live mixing, editing, timing, music scheduling, and transmission workflows matter because weak fundamentals compound as a production becomes more complex.\n\n## Creative intention meets production reality\n\nEntertainment production is a negotiation between ambition and constraints. Time, budget, access, performer availability, technology, rights, platform requirements, and audience expectations can all change what is possible. Constraints do not automatically reduce quality. Often they force a project to decide what matters most.\n\nThe strongest teams make those trade-offs explicit. They distinguish decisions that define the identity of the work from decisions that can change without damaging it. That distinction helps a production protect its central idea while still adapting to technical problems, schedule pressure, feedback, or distribution needs.\n\n## Collaboration and iteration\n\nCreative work becomes more reliable when feedback is attached to a clear objective. A note such as \"make it better\" is difficult to act on. A note about pacing, clarity, continuity, tone, audience understanding, technical quality, or a specific rights problem can be tested.\n\nIteration also changes with production stage. Early revisions are cheap and broad. Late revisions are usually narrower because more departments and assets depend on earlier choices. This is why experienced productions try to identify structural problems before expensive finishing work begins.\n\n## Evaluating the result\n\nFor radio production, useful evaluation criteria include clarity, timing, pacing, audio quality, audience flow, reliability, and compliance. These criteria do not replace artistic judgement. They make judgement more accountable by asking what the work is trying to achieve and whether the production choices support that aim.\n\nAudience response can add another layer of evidence, but popularity and quality are not identical. A project can be technically accomplished and reach a small audience, or attract a large audience while still containing craft problems. Good evaluation separates commercial outcomes, audience outcomes, and creative outcomes instead of treating them as one score.\n\n## Rights, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include broadcast standards, music licensing, safe levels, contributor consent, and emergency procedures. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nRadio production becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Radio production: What beginners often misunderstand","word_count":639,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Radio production"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"EBU","title":"EBU","url":"https://www.ebu.ch/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"entertainment-archive-2026-090","published_at":"2026-04-27T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"radio-production-how-production-evolves-over-time-090","title":"Radio production: How production evolves over time","description":"Radio production explained through how production evolves over time, with craft, production, evaluation, rights, safety, and audience context.","body_markdown":"# Radio production: How production evolves over time\n\nRadio production sits inside a larger entertainment system of creative intent, production craft, collaboration, technology, rights, distribution, and audience response. This article focuses on how production evolves over time while keeping those connections visible.\n\n## The core idea\n\nCreating audio programmes through scripting, presenting, recording, live control, editing, music, and transmission. Professional work rarely comes from one isolated decision. It emerges from a chain of choices that need to remain coherent as the project moves between people, tools, locations, and deadlines.\n\nThe practical vocabulary of radio production includes scripting, microphone technique, cueing, live mixing, editing, timing, music scheduling, and transmission workflows. These elements are useful because they let collaborators discuss the work precisely instead of relying only on taste or intuition.\n\n## How production evolves over time\n\nProduction evolves by moving from broad choices to increasingly specific ones. Early work defines intention and constraints, middle stages create and test material, and later stages refine continuity, quality, delivery, and audience fit. In radio production, experienced teams also build feedback loops so mistakes discovered late become better planning decisions on the next project.\n\n## Creative intention meets production reality\n\nEntertainment production is a negotiation between ambition and constraints. Time, budget, access, performer availability, technology, rights, platform requirements, and audience expectations can all change what is possible. Constraints do not automatically reduce quality. Often they force a project to decide what matters most.\n\nThe strongest teams make those trade-offs explicit. They distinguish decisions that define the identity of the work from decisions that can change without damaging it. That distinction helps a production protect its central idea while still adapting to technical problems, schedule pressure, feedback, or distribution needs.\n\n## Collaboration and iteration\n\nCreative work becomes more reliable when feedback is attached to a clear objective. A note such as \"make it better\" is difficult to act on. A note about pacing, clarity, continuity, tone, audience understanding, technical quality, or a specific rights problem can be tested.\n\nIteration also changes with production stage. Early revisions are cheap and broad. Late revisions are usually narrower because more departments and assets depend on earlier choices. This is why experienced productions try to identify structural problems before expensive finishing work begins.\n\n## Evaluating the result\n\nFor radio production, useful evaluation criteria include clarity, timing, pacing, audio quality, audience flow, reliability, and compliance. These criteria do not replace artistic judgement. They make judgement more accountable by asking what the work is trying to achieve and whether the production choices support that aim.\n\nAudience response can add another layer of evidence, but popularity and quality are not identical. A project can be technically accomplished and reach a small audience, or attract a large audience while still containing craft problems. Good evaluation separates commercial outcomes, audience outcomes, and creative outcomes instead of treating them as one score.\n\n## Rights, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include broadcast standards, music licensing, safe levels, contributor consent, and emergency procedures. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nRadio production becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Radio production: How production evolves over time","word_count":642,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Radio production"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"EBU","title":"EBU","url":"https://www.ebu.ch/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"entertainment-archive-2026-091","published_at":"2026-04-28T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"radio-production-what-rights-and-safety-standards-protect-091","title":"Radio production: What rights and safety standards protect","description":"Radio production explained through what rights and safety standards protect, with craft, production, evaluation, rights, safety, and audience context.","body_markdown":"# Radio production: What rights and safety standards protect\n\nRadio production sits inside a larger entertainment system of creative intent, production craft, collaboration, technology, rights, distribution, and audience response. This article focuses on what rights and safety standards protect while keeping those connections visible.\n\n## The core idea\n\nCreating audio programmes through scripting, presenting, recording, live control, editing, music, and transmission. Professional work rarely comes from one isolated decision. It emerges from a chain of choices that need to remain coherent as the project moves between people, tools, locations, and deadlines.\n\nThe practical vocabulary of radio production includes scripting, microphone technique, cueing, live mixing, editing, timing, music scheduling, and transmission workflows. These elements are useful because they let collaborators discuss the work precisely instead of relying only on taste or intuition.\n\n## What rights and safety standards protect\n\nRights and safety standards protect both people and the creative work. Important risks in radio production include broadcast standards, music licensing, safe levels, contributor consent, and emergency procedures. Good production practice therefore treats contracts, permissions, credits, consent, workplace safety, accessibility, and documented approvals as part of the creative process rather than paperwork added at the end.\n\n## Creative intention meets production reality\n\nEntertainment production is a negotiation between ambition and constraints. Time, budget, access, performer availability, technology, rights, platform requirements, and audience expectations can all change what is possible. Constraints do not automatically reduce quality. Often they force a project to decide what matters most.\n\nThe strongest teams make those trade-offs explicit. They distinguish decisions that define the identity of the work from decisions that can change without damaging it. That distinction helps a production protect its central idea while still adapting to technical problems, schedule pressure, feedback, or distribution needs.\n\n## Collaboration and iteration\n\nCreative work becomes more reliable when feedback is attached to a clear objective. A note such as \"make it better\" is difficult to act on. A note about pacing, clarity, continuity, tone, audience understanding, technical quality, or a specific rights problem can be tested.\n\nIteration also changes with production stage. Early revisions are cheap and broad. Late revisions are usually narrower because more departments and assets depend on earlier choices. This is why experienced productions try to identify structural problems before expensive finishing work begins.\n\n## Evaluating the result\n\nFor radio production, useful evaluation criteria include clarity, timing, pacing, audio quality, audience flow, reliability, and compliance. These criteria do not replace artistic judgement. They make judgement more accountable by asking what the work is trying to achieve and whether the production choices support that aim.\n\nAudience response can add another layer of evidence, but popularity and quality are not identical. A project can be technically accomplished and reach a small audience, or attract a large audience while still containing craft problems. Good evaluation separates commercial outcomes, audience outcomes, and creative outcomes instead of treating them as one score.\n\n## Rights, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include broadcast standards, music licensing, safe levels, contributor consent, and emergency procedures. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nRadio production becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Radio production: What rights and safety standards protect","word_count":647,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Radio production"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"EBU","title":"EBU","url":"https://www.ebu.ch/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"entertainment-archive-2026-092","published_at":"2026-04-29T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"podcast-production-how-it-works-092","title":"Podcast production: How it works","description":"Podcast production explained through how it works, with craft, production, evaluation, rights, safety, and audience context.","body_markdown":"# Podcast production: How it works\n\nPodcast production sits inside a larger entertainment system of creative intent, production craft, collaboration, technology, rights, distribution, and audience response. This article focuses on how it works while keeping those connections visible.\n\n## The core idea\n\nBuilding episodic audio through concept development, research, recording, editing, hosting, distribution, and audience development. Professional work rarely comes from one isolated decision. It emerges from a chain of choices that need to remain coherent as the project moves between people, tools, locations, and deadlines.\n\nThe practical vocabulary of podcast production includes format design, research, interviewing, microphone technique, editing, loudness, metadata, publishing, and promotion. These elements are useful because they let collaborators discuss the work precisely instead of relying only on taste or intuition.\n\n## How it works\n\nBuilding episodic audio through concept development, research, recording, editing, hosting, distribution, and audience development. The process works by turning a creative intention into a sequence of decisions, assets, rehearsals, technical checks, and revisions. Format design, research, interviewing, microphone technique, editing, loudness, metadata, publishing, and promotion are the working tools of that process.\n\n## Creative intention meets production reality\n\nEntertainment production is a negotiation between ambition and constraints. Time, budget, access, performer availability, technology, rights, platform requirements, and audience expectations can all change what is possible. Constraints do not automatically reduce quality. Often they force a project to decide what matters most.\n\nThe strongest teams make those trade-offs explicit. They distinguish decisions that define the identity of the work from decisions that can change without damaging it. That distinction helps a production protect its central idea while still adapting to technical problems, schedule pressure, feedback, or distribution needs.\n\n## Collaboration and iteration\n\nCreative work becomes more reliable when feedback is attached to a clear objective. A note such as \"make it better\" is difficult to act on. A note about pacing, clarity, continuity, tone, audience understanding, technical quality, or a specific rights problem can be tested.\n\nIteration also changes with production stage. Early revisions are cheap and broad. Late revisions are usually narrower because more departments and assets depend on earlier choices. This is why experienced productions try to identify structural problems before expensive finishing work begins.\n\n## Evaluating the result\n\nFor podcast production, useful evaluation criteria include listener retention, clarity, consistency, sound quality, episode structure, discoverability, and trust. These criteria do not replace artistic judgement. They make judgement more accountable by asking what the work is trying to achieve and whether the production choices support that aim.\n\nAudience response can add another layer of evidence, but popularity and quality are not identical. A project can be technically accomplished and reach a small audience, or attract a large audience while still containing craft problems. Good evaluation separates commercial outcomes, audience outcomes, and creative outcomes instead of treating them as one score.\n\n## Rights, safety, and professional practice\n\nEntertainment work also carries obligations beyond the finished artefact. Risks can include music rights, defamation, privacy, source consent, advertising disclosure, and data collection. Strong production practice documents ownership, permissions, credits, approvals, and safety responsibilities early enough that they can shape decisions rather than become emergencies.\n\nAccessibility belongs in the same conversation. Captions, audio description, readable graphics, navigable digital interfaces, venue access, and other accommodations can expand who is able to experience the work and can improve clarity for wider audiences.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPodcast production becomes easier to understand when craft, production systems, audience experience, rights, and safety are treated as connected. The most durable creative organisations learn from each project, document what worked, and improve the process as deliberately as they improve the final work.\n\nThis is an evergreen Blogdel entertainment explainer. Exact rules, contracts, technical standards, and platform requirements can change, so current primary documentation should be checked when a real production decision depends on exact wording.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/entertainment-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Podcast production: How it works","word_count":635,"reading_time_minutes":3,"keywords":["entertainment","evergreen","explainer","Podcast production"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"provider":"AES","title":"AES","url":"https://www.aes.org/","authority":"primary","position":0},{"provider":"UNESCO","title":"UNESCO Culture","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/culture","authority":"primary","position":1},{"provider":"WIPO","title":"World Intellectual Property Organization","url":"https://www.wipo.int/copyright/en/","authority":"primary","position":2}]},{"external_id":"entertainment-archive-2026-093","published_at":"2026-04-30T12:00:00.000Z","slug":"podcast-production-why-craft-matters-093","title":"Podcast production: Why craft matters","description":"Podcast production explained through why craft matters, with craft, production, evaluation, rights, safety, and audience context.","body_markdown":"# Podcast production: Why craft matters\n\nPodcast production sits inside a larger entertainment system of creative intent, production craft, collaboration, technology, rights, distribution, and audience response. This article focuses on why craft matters while keeping those connections visible.\n\n## The core idea\n\nBuilding episodic audio through concept development, research, recording, editing, hosting, distribution, and audience development. Professional work rarely comes from one i